# CLI & wiring

## The `Migrate`/`db` wiring in `core/db.py`

```python
from flask import Flask
from flask_migrate import Migrate
from flask_sqlalchemy import SQLAlchemy

db = SQLAlchemy()
migrate = Migrate()


def init_db(app: Flask, *, testing: bool = False) -> None:
    ...
    db.init_app(app)
    # `migrate.init_app` is what registers the `flask db ...` CLI command
    # group at all — without it `flask db migrate`/`upgrade` don't exist.
    migrate.init_app(app, db)
    ...
```

Both `db` and `migrate` are module-level singletons, constructed empty
and bound to the real `app` later inside `init_db()` — the same
deferred-initialization shape Flask-SQLAlchemy itself uses, and for the
same reason: importing `core/db.py` (which `flask db ...`, `pytest`, and
plain tooling all do) must never require a live app or perform I/O.
`migrate.init_app(app, db)` is the step that actually registers the `db`
command group on the Flask CLI — until it runs, `flask db migrate` isn't
a recognized command at all, which is why every `flask db ...` call
needs `--app src/{{ package_name }}/main.py`: that's what makes Flask
build the app (and, inside it, run `init_db()`) before dispatching to
the command.

`migrate.init_app` takes the Flask-SQLAlchemy `db` object as its second
argument on purpose — that's the object it reads model metadata and the
engine from to do anything. There's no version of this wiring that
doesn't need a Flask-SQLAlchemy `db`; a `Migrate()` with no `db` has
nothing to diff against.

## What each `flask db` command does

| Command | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `flask db migrate -m "..."` | Autogenerate: diffs the current models against the last-applied revision and writes a new file under `migrations/versions/`. Nothing is applied to any database yet — this only writes a file. |
| `flask db upgrade` | Applies every unapplied revision, in order, up to the latest (`head`). This is what actually changes the database schema. |
| `flask db downgrade` | Reverts the single most recently applied revision (runs its `downgrade()`). Run it again to go back further. |

All three are run through `uv run flask --app src/{{ package_name }}/main.py db <command>`
in this project — see `guides/first-migration.md` for the sequence.

## `migrations/env.py` and `alembic.ini`: generated vs. customized

Both files are Flask-Migrate's own boilerplate (`flask db init` would
generate the same ones) — flint ships them pre-generated rather than
running `init` at generation time, but the content is untouched:

- **`migrations/env.py`** pulls the engine and model metadata off
  `current_app.extensions['migrate'].db` — that's the live `db` object
  `migrate.init_app(app, db)` registered, which is why `env.py` needs a
  real Flask app context (via `--app`) to run at all, unlike plain
  Alembic's `env.py`, which usually imports a `Base`/engine directly.
  It also installs a `process_revision_directives` callback that
  suppresses writing an empty migration file when there's genuinely
  nothing to diff — the actual mechanism behind "No changes in schema
  detected" (see `references/gotchas.md`).
- **`alembic.ini`** is mostly logging configuration (loggers for
  `alembic`, `sqlalchemy`, and `flask_migrate`) — it deliberately has no
  `sqlalchemy.url` set, because `env.py` fills that in at runtime from
  the live app's `SQLALCHEMY_DATABASE_URI` instead. Don't hand-edit a
  URL into this file; it would be dead configuration, silently
  overridden every time a `flask db` command runs.

Everything else about how autogenerate diffing works and how a
migration file's `upgrade()`/`downgrade()` pair is structured is plain
Alembic — see the `alembic` skill for that, it's identical here.
